<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/19th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty</p><h1 class="enc-title">Clayton–Bulwer Treaty</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1850">1850</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Clayton–Bulwer Treaty was signed in 1850 by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, represented by John M. Clayton and Sir Henry Bulwer. Both powers agreed that neither would seek exclusive control of a future canal across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-america">Central America</a> or colonise any part of the region. Disputes over its meaning led Britain to give up the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bay-islands">Bay Islands</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mosquito-coast">Mosquito Coast</a>, and it was replaced by the Hay–Pauncefote Treaty of 1901.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/clayton-bulwer-treaty.jpg" alt="Clayton–Bulwer Treaty" width="320" height="387" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Giuseppe Fagnani · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Giuseppe_Fagnani_(1819-1873)_-_William_Bulwer,_Baron_Dalling_and_Bulwer_-_NPG_852_-_National_Portrait_Gallery.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The treaty was negotiated amid growing tension over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-america">Central America</a>, where British influence was traditionally strong but American expansion was increasing, and over American plans for a canal through Nicaragua joining the Pacific and the Atlantic. Britain had indefinite territorial claims in British Honduras, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mosquito-coast">Mosquitia</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bay-islands">Bay Islands</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> made no claims but held in reserve treaties with Nicaragua and Honduras that gave it a diplomatic counterweight. As agreement on territory proved impossible, the negotiators put the canal first.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Terms</h2><p>Signed on 19 April 1850, the treaty bound both parties not to obtain or maintain exclusive control of the proposed canal or unequal advantage in its use, guaranteed its neutrality, and extended their protection to any other practicable route across the isthmus, by canal or railway. Neither would occupy, fortify, colonise or exercise dominion over Nicaragua, Costa Rica, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mosquito-coast">Mosquito Coast</a> or any part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-america">Central America</a>, nor use a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a> or alliance to that end.</p><p>Before ratifications were exchanged, Lord Palmerston had Bulwer declare on 8 June that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> did not take the treaty to apply to its settlement at Honduras or its dependencies. Clayton's counter-declaration accepted the exception only for British Honduras and the small islands near it, held that the treaty covered all the Central American states within their just limits, and stated that the declarations, not put before the Senate, did not affect its legality.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> held that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bay-islands">Bay Islands</a> were a dependency of Belize and that it need not give up its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a> over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mosquito-coast">Mosquito Coast</a>; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> denied both, and in 1852 Britain broke the treaty by making the Bay Islands a colony. Treaties of 1859 and 1860 settled the questions nearly in accord with the American contentions: Britain ceded the Bay Islands and the northern Mosquito Coast to Honduras by the Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/comayagua">Comayagua</a> and the rest of the coast to Nicaragua by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-managua">Treaty of Managua</a>.</p><p>In 1880–1884 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> advanced reasons for repudiating the treaty at will, among them that circumstances had changed fundamentally since it was signed, the first time its government invoked that principle in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/international-law">international law</a>. It returned to its traditional policy in 1885, and the canal was never built under the treaty.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Clayton–Bulwer Treaty” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The prospect of a Nicaraguan canal shaped British and American rivalry in the Caribbean, producing the <b>Clayton–Bulwer Treaty</b> of 1850, and the route remained the main alternative to Panama until the United States Senate chose Panama in 1902.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-nicaragua">Republic of Nicaragua · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The United States protested that the colony breached the <b>Clayton-Bulwer Treaty</b> of 1850, in which both powers had agreed not to colonise Central America.</blockquote><a href="/state/colony-of-the-bay-islands">Colony of the Bay Islands</a></li></ul></section>
